Kamalie future workspace integration (@kamalie-workspace-integration: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @ws-kamalie-games) {
Historical architecture exploration preserved from the original Record. Standalone-castle.lingua and Vibe-and-runs.lingua are the current game direction; they supersede earlier suggestions for game-native UI, undecided viewpoint and active-skill examples.

## Planned capabilities that help build games

The following selection contains 26 distinct feature packages, grouped by their contribution. The count describes reusable ingredients. Each game uses a suitable subset and consumes the verified exports it actually needs.

| Planned packages | Contribution | Game examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Geometry](../features/Geometry.lingua), [Caves](../features/Caves.lingua), [Navigation](../features/Navigation.lingua), [Simplification](../features/Simplification.lingua) | Local rooms, editable terrain, movement queries and bounded support/collision representations | Dungeon crawlers, exploration, platformers |
| [Frames](../features/Frames.lingua), [Areas and mounts](../features/Areas-Mounts.lingua), [Mounted apps](../features/Mounted-Apps.lingua) | Coordinates, regions, influence, solid panels, spatial content and HUD views | Workspace arenas, survivors, physics puzzles |
| [Object recipes](../features/Object-Recipes.lingua), [Functional behavior](../features/Functional.lingua), [Behavior authoring](../features/Behavior-Authoring.lingua), [Procedural appearance](../features/Procedural-Appearance.lingua), [Authoring experiments](../features/Authoring-Experiments.lingua) | Reusable definitions, typed actions, state/timers, effects and controlled trials | Build crafting, interactive traps, automation and puzzle games |
| [Possession](../features/Possession.lingua), [Object controls](../features/Object-Control-Bindings.lingua) | Movement and action intents, remapping, controller ownership and release | Action games, vehicles, controllable objects |
| [Character starter](../features/Character-Starter.lingua), [Character rig](../features/Character-Rig.lingua), [Character motion](../features/Character-Motion.lingua), [Character integration](../features/Character-Integration.lingua) | Supplied actors, animation, authored controllers and detail policy | Third-person action, creature games, animated RPGs |
| [Rigid bodies](../features/Rigid.lingua), [Physical articulation](../features/Physical-Articulation.lingua), [Mechanical transitions](../features/Mechanical-Transitions.lingua) | Richer contacts, joints and supported cheap/detailed model substitutions | Mechanism puzzles, construction and physical simulation games |
| [Hosting](../features/Hosting.lingua), [Shared presence](../features/Shared-Presence.lingua) | Access, scoped queries, shared identity and authoritative edits | Foundations for cooperative worlds and collaborative level building |
| [Agent context](../features/Agent-Context.lingua), [Agent authoring](../features/Agent-Authoring.lingua), [Recording](../features/Recording.lingua) | Fiote-assisted inspection/authoring and optional supported playback | AI-assisted creation, encounter analysis and authored challenges |

Shared Model, Assets, Selection and other foundations remain prerequisites where named by those packages. Additional optional ingredients include CAD and assemblies for construction games, schematics and circuits for redstone-like logic, molecules and materials for scientific puzzles, geography for location-based games, and camera/vision adapters for real-world-responsive scenes. Logic construction can start with bounded digital behaviors. Detailed circuit or molecular simulation can be enabled for an experience that actually uses it.

Shared hosting supplies infrastructure for multiplayer. Authoritative combat ticks, prediction, reconciliation, reconnect, rollback and adversarial input handling require a separate gameplay networking design. Fiote can author or direct encounters through supported actions while ordinary enemy steering runs in bounded game systems.

## Highly customizable builds

Separate the game-specific operators from their authoring presentation. Kamalie needs implemented targeting, resource costs, cooldowns, damage/status rules, spawn limits, projectiles, area effects, knockback and interruption. Discover and implement those mechanics directly in the isolated Bevy game, with game-native configuration and controls. Object recipes and behavior authoring can subsequently expose the useful operators as reusable editable definitions with appearance bindings.

For example, one ability can choose a cone target shape, emit three bouncing projectiles, apply a slow effect on hit and shorten a cooldown when a defeated target satisfies a predicate. Another can apply an attraction field, turn absorbed projectiles into a resource and spend it on a defensive action. Their visuals, timing and public parameters remain editable independently of the internal geometry.

Define stacking order, modifier precedence, target categories, recursion limits, trigger frequency, resource accounting and cancellation. Feedback such as on-hit spawning another hit must have a bounded policy. Native compiled systems execute the operators, while game definitions choose values and composition. The optional standalone runner can load the same game definition schema and use its own native controls. Lince's later recipe editor integration adapts those definitions through the shared authoring path.

Ship a small useful set of actual operators and build combinations before generalizing an editor. Record behavior revision and active-state migration rules when a build changes mid-run. Preserve live custom shader and compiled system extension points within the host's supported engine configuration. CAD can later supply a weapon's authored geometry and sockets when that experience needs them.

## Contained dungeon mode — first product form

The Castle owns an ordinary workspace rectangle and a game viewport. The dungeon has a local coordinate frame, map, character, enemies and camera. Character input changes the game character and camera. Normal workspace editing moves the host rectangle through the existing placement path.

Render the game camera into a GPU image or a supported clipped camera viewport. The GPU image approach fits transformed or spatially mounted content. Reuse host images/materials and measure composition, target resizing, resolution scaling and demand. Establish input coordinates through the actual crop, aspect ratio, transform and device scale. Bevy provides a render-to-texture example using a camera target and visibility layers. [Bevy render-to-texture example](https://bevy.org/examples/3d-rendering/render-to-texture/)

The game's local map supplies its environment, starting with a small authored room and adding generation as the encounter design develops. The game can use its complete movement/combat/build implementation in this mode. A HUD can use native Bevy UI initially and later bind supported Lince views to the same observable game state. Closing the view, stopping the run and deleting the Castle need separate lifecycle actions. A hidden or unfocused session follows its declared pause/background policy and releases input immediately on focus loss.

Dungeon crawling describes map structure, encounters and navigation. It can use real-time combat. A traditional turn-based variant can share abilities, inventory, effect resolution and content while providing a separate action-cost scheduler and time semantics. Retrofitting every timer by pausing rendered frames would leave cooldown, AI and external input behavior ambiguous. Define turn-based support as its own tested rule profile if selected.

## Workspace-area mode — subsequent integration

Begin this branch after the isolated Castle milestone is usable and measured. Preserve the direct Bevy game implementation and add the concrete environment/interaction adapters it needs. Workspace-specific mechanics can extend that implementation as the integrated game develops.

### Session and participant configuration

Place a Kamalie Castle, bind or draw its region, select the environment policy and start the session. Focusing the game supplies controls to an already admitted session. Area membership selects candidates through the existing scoped query path. Current grants and movement ownership determine which effects can execute. The existing R0245 access rule continues to apply. [Durable authority and visibility](../contracts/Durable-model-contracts.lingua)

An initial region preset can enroll existing eligible panels as solid scenery and selected movable objects as bodies. Each occurrence has declared participation roles. A surface may provide more than one role when the ownership rules support it.

| Role | Game behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Scenery | Visible context with authored collision participation |
| Obstacle or platform | A bounded collision/support shape with material and one-way/solid policy where supported |
| Movable scenery | A body with explicit mass, constraints and one runtime pose owner |
| Game actor | Game-defined health, faction, targeting and damage responses |
| Interaction surface | A usable app or Castle with mapped pointer/keyboard focus |
| HUD content | A view-relative presentation with preserved source identity and lifecycle |

World membership and simulation locality need explicit policies for entering/leaving the region, nested Areas, overlapping sessions, external force fields and moving the region itself. A game-owned projectile's exit policy can despawn, clamp or transfer through a supported boundary. Shared props keep their existing physical interactions according to the selected participation contract. A closed arena that confines every physical consequence needs a real isolated domain or constraints on all relevant contacts. Spatial query filtering alone cannot guarantee confinement when an enrolled body collides with objects outside it.

For the first live version, each movable participant has one active motion controller/session. Overlapping games can share observation while competing writes resolve through the existing ownership path. Region movement changes the query boundary by default. Moving an entire bound scene is a separate transform operation with the normal constraints and validation.

### Firefox as a platform or movable object

Park continues to own Firefox's real surface and application lifecycle. The adapter publishes its selected occurrence's display shape, pose, local hit coordinates and optional physics body. A simple rectangle or slab can be a support surface. Jumping needs a controller with grounding, step/ceiling handling and a declared dimensional model. The 2D canvas can use an explicit virtual height or platformer plane, while a spatial view can use a 3D slab.

The collision shape can be much cheaper than the visible live application. Browser resizing, workspace dragging, mounting, deletion and source reconnection update or release that shape at safe boundaries. Its authored interaction mass comes from the game's object policy. Page content acquires game meaning only through a separate supported app/content adapter.

Live movement sends forces or controller intents to the chosen body owner and reflects its resulting pose into the actual panel presentation. Existing layout, pinning, mounts, dragging and authoring controls must participate in that ownership handoff. User manipulation can suspend or transfer the game's control through a visible normal action. Game damage updates game state. Closing an application, deleting its source or issuing a real command uses its separately supported Lince action.

### Video call and other Castles as HUD

Bind an existing content occurrence to a view-relative HUD mount with a layout region and input policy. Preserve its app/client identity, audio/media demand and normal lifecycle. Reuse the planned spatial-to-flat/HUD presentation boundary. If simultaneous world and HUD views are supported, both refer to the same content owner and use an explicit input focus policy. [Mounted app task 07.T03](../features/Mounted-Apps.lingua)

Other Castles can provide inventory, quest, timer, build selection or status UI through real typed actions and observable state. A compatible Castle binds to those game interfaces. Its existing visual composition alone does not implement an inventory or combat action. Preserve accessibility, readable controls and the normal edit panel's usable bounds under Lince's edit-panel-geometry rule. [Native interface requirements](../../../Lince.lingua)

### Input, time and state ownership

Route keyboard, pointer and gamepad input to the focused game, application interaction, HUD control or workspace tool. Switching to Firefox typing releases game movement. Returning to the game restores the selected controller without replaying held keys. Provide a reliable release action and pointer-capture recovery. Camera navigation must consume the active control context so one key press cannot move both the workspace camera and the player unintentionally.

Pause applies to the session's owned state. A video call and other live applications keep their own clocks and lifecycle. A session that shares physical bodies with the live workspace must define how paused game actors interact with those bodies. Independent slow motion or stepping requires isolated runtime state or an explicitly supported shared-clock policy.

Keep definition edits, run state, runtime participant poses and accepted workspace placement separately owned. A new run resets its owned gameplay state. Runtime movement can remain an activity pose while the game is active, with selected final placements adopted through ordinary validated commands when requested. Restore or release against the current accepted workspace revision so concurrent user edits survive. Durable run saves must capture supported state and versions. A seed alone cannot reconstruct all live app events, concurrent edits or physics outcomes.

Existing Possession POS.T03 defines Play as a bounded temporary trial with a narrow Keep pose operation. Kamalie's live area session needs an explicit additional activity adapter. Preserve that existing trial workflow and use its intent/release mechanisms where their contracts fit. Starting the configured live session can grant its declared scope once through the existing action path. Per-frame control uses the admitted session. [Possession](../features/Possession.lingua)

### Attraction and Karma

Bind admitted Area field samples or forces into movement/physics through existing filters, units, immunity and state ownership. An attraction ability can alter a game-owned field or invoke a supported scoped field action. Resolve membership efficiently and update only affected participants.

Karma can supply configured predicates, progression events or automation through explicit adapters. Cache or compile supported authored rules at the definition boundary and use bounded event-level calls where appropriate. Game score and game resources have their own state definitions. Any outcome intended to change actual Lince operational history enters the usual authorized, idempotent action path. Retrying or loading a run must resolve an already-issued outcome before emitting it again.

## Decision to revisit after the first proof

The first product route is the isolated native Castle using direct Bevy gameplay. Its normal placement/focus workflow, playtesting and measured optimization come first. Use the first small host experiment to choose a sound execution boundary, and refine it using KAM.P00 and the two-instance test. The optional runner supports development. Once the game has useful mechanics, introduce the area adapter and shared authoring integrations through the later tasks. Preserve the full capability map and technical context here so those extensions can reuse the game deliberately as its design becomes clearer.

## Original product exploration


A game combines capabilities, content, rules and initial configuration. Minecraft's redstone computers illustrate how a configurable system can contain other systems and games. Lince's composition, spatial interaction and authored behavior can support that breadth. Kamalie is a concrete consumer that can expose reusable gaps in those capabilities.

The owner wants two usable forms of the same gameplay. A contained dungeon runs within a window, screen or Castle. A workspace-area version uses an authored region and participating components as scenery, physical objects, controls or HUD. Firefox can be a platform or movable panel, a video call can remain visible in the HUD, and existing Castles can supply UI. The area version can additionally use Lince attraction fields and supported Karma operations.

The selected starting point is a self-contained native Castle containing Bevy-native gameplay. The gameplay library owns ordinary Bevy components and systems. A small Lince adapter supplies the outer element's placement, presentation, focus and lifecycle. An optional standalone runner helps development and measurement. The area adapter follows the first playable Castle. A simple 2D or 2.5D example remains one candidate for the first experiment. Art style, viewpoint, combat pacing and turn-based support remain product choices.

}
